//? Title: Non-restoring unsigned division
//? Subtitle: Actual signed registers, final correction and honest arithmetic counts
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Compute exact64-bit quotient and remainder while carrying a signed partial
remainder through one add or subtract per bit. Actual native register histories
replace the old report-side simulator. The original divisions and six-dividend
work study are retained, with the final correction included and the
comparison-first software implementation distinguished from a hypothetical
subtract-and-restore circuit model.
)

\toc

> Carry an overshoot into the next bit

The canonical routine keeps a signed remainder A and a64-bit register Q,
initially A=0 and Q=dividend. Each of64 stages transfers Q’s old
most-significant bit into A by A←2A+incoming, shifts Q modulo2⁶⁴ and then
subtracts D if A is nonnegative or adds D if A is negative. The new sign
determines Q’s low quotient bit. A final negative A receives one addition of D.
That final correction is actual work, even though no per-stage over-subtraction
is immediately restored.

For D>0, the post-stage invariant−D≤A<D keeps the next shifted value within65
magnitude bits. Signed128 arithmetic is therefore sufficient for every
unsigned64-bit input. The producer uses multiplication by2 rather than a left
shift of a negative signed value. At completion the corrected remainder lies
in[0,D), and the emitted quotient satisfies N=QD+R. Native exact full-width
checks verify that identity; the report does not infer correctness from a
plotted approximate register value.

> Inspect the last four actual stages of13/3

The routine still executes64 stages. The diagram shows only its actual last four
records, after the leading zero dividend bits have been processed. These are not
a separate four-bit algorithm. Exact decimal signed registers come from native
high/low magnitude records. The actual final correction turns A=−2 into
remainder1, giving quotient4.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/nonrestoring_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto t = dm::algorithms::non_restoring_division_trace(13, 3);
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    std::print("| actual stage index | original bit | A before | A shifted | operation | A after | quotient " "output bit |\n| ---: | ---: | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t i = 60; i < t.steps.size(); ++i) {
        auto &s = t.steps[i];
        auto before =
            dm::algorithms::division_wide_decimal(s.before), shift =
            dm::algorithms::division_wide_decimal(s.shifted), after =
            dm::algorithms::division_wide_decimal(s.after);
        std::print("| {} | {} | `{}` | `{}` | {} | `{}` | {} |\n", i, s.bit,
                   before, shift, s.subtracted ? "subtract3" : "add3", after,
                   int(s.output_bit));
        d.node(std::to_string(i), {6. * (i - 60), 0},
               std::format("Actual stage {}\nA={} / output {}", i, after,
                           int(s.output_bit)));
        if (i > 60)
            d.edge(std::to_string(i - 1), std::to_string(i));
    }
    std::print("\nActual full64-stage result: quotient {}, remainder {}, final correction {}.\n",
               t.analysis.result.quotient, t.analysis.result.remainder,
               t.final_corrected);
    d.save_svg("build/nonrestoring_tiny.svg");
}
```
